Consider the standard framework of human complex-trait genetics, in which a quantitative or binary phenotype Y measured across a population is modeled as a function of genetic variation G and non-genetic factors E, and in which "narrow-sense heritability" h² denotes the fraction of phenotypic variance attributable to additive genetic effects. Most large-scale association and heritability studies represent each individual's genome as a vector of deviations (single-nucleotide polymorphisms and short indels) from a single linear reference genome, restricted largely to autosomes and to variants that are easily called by short-read sequencing.

Several classes of genetic variation are systematically underrepresented or absent from this representation, including:
(i) variation on the sex chromosomes X and Y;
(ii) mitochondrial DNA variation;
(iii) large structural variants and copy-number variants;
(iv) variation in repetitive regions (e.g., tandem repeats, segmental duplications, centromeric and telomeric regions);
(v) haplotype phase information;
(vi) somatic mosaicism (genetic differences between cells within an individual);
(vii) sequence and structural content not representable as deviations from a single reference, such as that captured by pangenome graphs or personal de novo assemblies.

Quantify, for a representative set of human complex traits, the contribution of each of the above classes of variation to total narrow-sense heritability, and determine the extent to which they collectively account for the gap ("missing heritability") between pedigree-based estimates of h² and the heritability captured by current genome-wide association studies. Furthermore, propose and justify statistical models and measurement protocols (sequencing, variant calling, encoding, and association testing) under which each of these forms of variation can be incorporated into genetic studies, and decide in particular whether somatic mosaicism is best modeled as a heritable genetic component or treated as a non-genetic (environmental/omic) factor.

